messages:
sara-support: FYI escalation from Acme. Customer sends embeddings request and gets 400: "invalid input shape". Happening only when they send single item payloads, works with arrays.

tony-se: Can you paste the exact request body they used?

sara-support: They attached this snippet from their Node client: ```js
const body = { input: [0.12, -0.03, ...] }
fetch('/v1/embeddings', { method: 'POST', body: JSON.stringify(body) })
```

mike-ml: Ah yeah that's the classic single-vs-batch issue. Our embeddings endpoint expects input to be a list of items, each item either a string or a numeric vector when using raw vectors. If you send a raw numeric vector directly (not wrapped in an outer list) the server assumes wrong schema. Error text: "invalid input shape: expected list of items".

tony-se: Quick repro I did: curl -X POST /v1/embeddings -H 'Authorization: Bearer $KEY' -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"input": [0.1,0.2,0.3]}' -> 400. Same payload wrapped -> '{"input": [[0.1,0.2,0.3]]}' -> 200.

infra-bot: [deploy-log] route: /v1/embeddings, version: embedding-service-v3, request_id: r_abc123, status: 400, error_code: INVALID_INPUT_SHAPE

nicole-cs: Customer says they switched from our old client to a tiny homegrown wrapper in Node. Likely missed batch wrapping. Asking if we can provide a clear error message they can parse.

mike-ml: Two suggestions: (1) server returns 400 with message pointing to expected shape, but we can add example and a short hint. (2) add a quick client-side guard snippet we can share.

tony-se: I'll paste a tiny guard for Node/Python we use in support docs. Node: ```js
function normalizeInput(x){
  // if user passes a single string or vector, wrap it into an array
  if (!Array.isArray(x)) return [x];
  // check if it's a vector (array of numbers) vs batch (array of items)
  if (x.length && typeof x[0] === 'number') return [x];
  return x;
}
const body = { input: normalizeInput(userInput) };
```
Python: ```py
def normalize_input(x):
    if not isinstance(x, list):
        return [x]
    if len(x) and isinstance(x[0], (int, float)):
        return [x]
    return x
```

sara-support: Also customer reported dimension mismatch after wrapping: they saw 422 saying expected dimension 1536 but got 128. They were accidentally sending a pre-computed 128-d vector from their library (not model-match).

mike-ml: Yes, two different problems: shape (batch vs single) and vector dimensionality. Recommend checking model config. Our embeddings model (rwd-embed-1536) produces 1536 dims. If customer is uploading externally computed vectors, they must match the model dims. We can return an explicit hint: "expected length 1536, got 128".

tony-se: Repro steps I gave them: 1) Confirm input is a list: `input: [[...]]` 2) If numeric vectors, check length: `len(input[0])` == expected 1536 3) If sending text, send strings, not token vectors 4) Add client guard.

nicole-cs: Customer asked if this could trigger billing spikes if they retry repeatedly. Answer: retries of 400/422 don't bill, but high-volume retries will count against rate limits. Also if they send huge batches with correct shape and keep retrying, that could affect quota.

infra-bot: [rate-limit] customer_id: acct_789, 24h retries detected: 512 attempts, most failed at 400. No quota breach yet.

tony-se: I'll prepare a short canned reply for CS with code snippets + exact error examples they can paste into logs. Also will open a small ticket to improve error details in the embeddings validator.

sara-support: Thanks. Please include suggested logging keys so the customer can grep: request_id, input_shape, input_dim_sample.

mike-ml: One more gotcha: some clients convert strings to token arrays or include metadata wrapper: `{ input: [{ text: "hello" }] }` is accepted, but `{ input: { text: "hello" } }` is not. Encourage them to validate JSON schema before sending.

tony-se: Will follow up with the customer now, copying the Node/Python snippets and the two checks (wrap + dim). :thumbsup:

nicole-cs: I'll tell the customer we'll patch error messages and keep them updated. :pray:

sara-support: Closing loop here, ping if they hit anything else.
